WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.407
Run this multi-CTE query and read it stage by stage.

00:00:04.407 --> 00:00:08.927
Admission counts has one row per patient with an admission count.

00:00:08.927 --> 00:00:14.907
Patient features joins that result to every patient and replaces missing counts with zero.

00:00:14.907 --> 00:00:18.552
The final SELECT classifies repeat versus single or none.

00:00:18.552 --> 00:00:23.192
Notice how naming intermediate results makes the logic easier to discuss.

00:00:23.192 --> 00:00:26.692
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:26.692 --> 00:00:34.607
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:34.607 --> 00:00:36.422
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:36.422 --> 00:00:43.572
In a real setting such as a sales team calculating running revenue and top products by region,

00:00:43.572 --> 00:00:49.272
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:49.272 --> 00:00:52.447
before discussing syntax.

00:00:52.447 --> 00:00:58.199
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
